   ]]></content:encoded></item><item><title><![CDATA[Friday discussion post]]></title><description><![CDATA[What I am reading and watching on 9/11]]></description><link>https://www.slowboring.com/p/friday-discussion-post-178</link><guid isPermaLink="false">https://www.slowboring.com/p/friday-discussion-post-178</guid><dc:creator><![CDATA[Halina Bennet]]></dc:creator><pubDate>Fri, 11 Sep 2026 22:01:04 GMT</pubDate><enclosure url="https://substack-post-media.s3.amazonaws.com/public/images/9a8be3ae-cf5b-41ab-8e64-89b9e193e425_2931x1535.jpeg" length="0" type="image/jpeg"/><content:encoded><![CDATA[<p>I was not even a year old on 9/11, so I don&#8217;t remember the terrorist attacks or any life before them. Still, like all Americans since that day, remembrance has been baked into my experience of the world. Here are a few of the pieces of media I have returned to over the years in an effort to understand the events, each of which I read or watched today:</p><ul><li><p>&#8220;<a href="https://www.nytimes.com/2002/05/26/nyregion/fighting-to-live-as-the-towers-died.html?unlocked_article_code=1._lA.U04p.8XSZivhybfBs&amp;smid=url-share">F&#8230;</a></p></li></ul>
